From 463db1568af7c05adce7c9cbbe23357705157207 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Garcia <44309076+ElGuslml@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:22:37 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 20/24] gpu: pair occlusion query ends with the active begin

The host occlusion queries of the D3D12 and Vulkan command processors
required the begin and the end EVENT_WRITE_ZPD to use the same
RB_SAMPLE_COUNT_ADDR, and turned host queries off for the rest of the
session on the first mismatch. The XDK Direct3D in Pirates of the
Caribbean: At World's End points the begin event at the second half of
the query's 64-byte block (block + 32) and the end event at the first
half, and GetData returns end - begin. So its first query turned host
queries off and every later one got the fake sample count: the game's
sun flare, whose strength is the visible fraction of a quad drawn at the
sun under the query, stayed at full strength with the sun behind the
camera or behind a wall.

With predicated tiling (the game's 2x MSAA scene is rendered in two
tiles) D3D gives each tile its own block, and each tile's pass begins
and ends the query on it, but it writes the 0xFFFFFEED end marker only
into the last tile's block. The first tile's end was taken for a begin
while a query was active, which also turned host queries off.

Pair the end with the begin whatever its address, take any event while
the guest's query is open as its end, zero the begin counts (the guest
subtracts them; the generic fallback path already did), and fall back
for an end without a host query only for that query instead of turning
host queries off. Whether the guest's query is open is tracked apart
from the host query, which EndSubmission ends early if a submission ends
in the middle of it; otherwise that query's end would be taken for a
begin, the following begin for an end, and a result would be written
into the begin counts.

The result was also written field by field while the guest may be
polling it: D3D takes a query as finished once ZPass_A or ZPass_B no
longer holds the marker and then adds both, so it could add a marker
left in ZPass_B. ZPass_A and ZPass_B are now written last, with one
8-byte store.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
